{
  "dom_id": "step-q35493",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Find the projection of the intersection between the hyperboloid of two sheets \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{y}^{2}-\\frac{(z+2)^{2}}{3}=-1$\nand the plane $3y+z=-2$  onto the\n$xy$-plane.",
  "worked_solution": "Since we want to find the projection onto the $xy$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $z.$ First, we solve the equation of the plane for $z$, as follows:\n\n$$\n3y+z=-2\\,⟹\\,z=-2-3y\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $z$ into the equation of the hyperboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{y}^{2}-\\frac{((-2-3y)+2)^{2}}{3} & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{y}^{2}-\\frac{9{y}^{2}}{3} & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-2{y}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4{y}^{2} & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection of the intersection is the hyperbola ${x}^{2}-4{y}^{2}=-2.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
